-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/65f911c9-c31e-494d-9f2b-f951876493f3
-- title:
--   The $n\times n$ lattice subsystem code is $[n^2,n,1]$ and corrects every error on $\le\lfloor (n-1)/2\rfloor$ qubits
-- statement:
--   Put $n^2$ qubits on an $n\times n$ square lattice, $n\ge1$. Let $\mathcal T$ be the gauge group (an even number of $X$'s in every column and of $Z$'s in every row), $\mathcal S\subseteq\mathcal T$ the stabilizer generated by the double rows $S^X_i$ of $X$ and double columns $S^Z_j$ of $Z$, and $C(\mathcal S)$ the Pauli operators commuting with $\mathcal S$. Let $\Pi_s$ be the projector onto the syndrome sector $s$ ($\Pi_0$ is the code sector), $Q(s)$ the recovery of (24) and its $X$ analogue (decoded from $s$, acting on a freely chosen column $j_0$ and row $i_0$), and $\bar X$, $\bar Z$ the logical operators of (25)–(26). Then:
--
--   1. **(one encoded qubit)** $\mathcal T\subseteq C(\mathcal S)$ and $|C(\mathcal S)| = 4\,|\mathcal T|$ (modulo phase);
--   2. **(distance $n$)** every Pauli operator in $C(\mathcal S)\setminus\mathcal T$ has weight at least $n$, and some Pauli operator in $C(\mathcal S)\setminus\mathcal T$ has weight exactly $n$;
--   3. **(correction)** for every syndrome $s$, all $i_0, j_0$, and every operator $E$ acting on at most $\lfloor (n-1)/2\rfloor$ qubits, the branch
--
--   $$
--   M = Q(s)\,\Pi_s\,E\,\Pi_0
--   $$
--
--   of "error $E$, measure syndrome $s$, apply $Q(s)$" satisfies $\Pi_0 M = M$, $M\bar X = \bar X M$ and $M\bar Z = \bar Z M$.
--
--   On the code sector $\mathcal H_0 = \mathcal H^{\mathcal T}\otimes\mathcal H^{\mathcal L}$ the operators commuting with $\bar X$ and $\bar Z$ are exactly those of the form $G\otimes I_2$. So clause 3 says that every branch of the recovery returns to the code sector and restores the encoded qubit, whatever it does to the gauge subsystem. This is the paper's "[$n^2$, $n$, 1] code … can correct up to $\lfloor (n-1)/2\rfloor$ arbitrary single qubit errors".
--
--   **Formalization Note** Clauses 1–2 work with Pauli operators modulo phase (pairs of bit matrices). Clause 3 uses the actual $2^{n^2}\times2^{n^2}$ matrices. "Acts on at most $t$ qubits" is the published `QAlgorithms.ActsOnAtMost t`, on the wires `site i j`.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: lattice size n, rows and columns indexed 1..n, generators indexed by Z_{n−1} → n = m + 1 with m : ℕ; rows and columns Fin (m + 1), 0-based; generators indexed by Fin m acting on i.castSucc, i.succ; reason: Lean convention; n = 0 has no qubits, so every lattice of the paper is an instance
--   - encoding: Pauli operators with phases ±1, ±i (the (−1)^φ of (8), (13)) → pairs (a, b) of bit matrices modulo phase; multiplication is addition, commutation is the symplectic bit symp; reason: membership in T, S, C(S), commutation and weight do not depend on the phase
--   - reading: S as the set (10): ⊕_i(∧_j a_ij) = 0, ⊕_j(∧_i b_ij) = 0 → S = the group generated by S^X_i, S^Z_j of (11) (SGroup m); reason: (10) taken alone admits non-full rows (n = 2: a single X_{1,1} satisfies it but anticommutes with Z_{1,1}Z_{1,2} ∈ T, contrary to p.4); the prose before (10) and the generators (11) agree, and S_props proves that the span equals the prose set
--   - encoding: syndrome eigenvalues s^X_i, s^Z_i = ±1; code sector s^X = s^Z = {+1}^{n−1} → syndrome bits in ZMod 2, bit 0 ↔ eigenvalue +1, bit 1 ↔ −1; code sector s = 0; reason: Lean convention; the same labels
--   - encoding: f″ = the one of f′, ¬f′ with the smallest Hamming weight (tie unspecified) → repDecode returns f′ on a tie H(f′) = H(¬f′); reason: the page leaves ties open; a tie needs H(f) = n/2 > ⌊(n−1)/2⌋, so no instance within the correction radius changes
--   - reading: "it encodes a single qubit into n² qubits" → |C(S)| = 4·|T| (modulo phase), with T ⊆ C(S); reason: for a subsystem code with gauge group T and stabilizer S the number k of logical qubits is given by |C(S)|/|T| = 4^k
--   - reading: "has a distance n" → every element of C(S) \ T has weight ≥ n, and one has weight exactly n; reason: the distance of a subsystem code is the minimum weight of an operator commuting with S but not in the gauge group; both halves of "exactly n" are kept
--   - reading: "correct … modulo the subsystem structure" (p.7): restore H^L, any action on H^T allowed → for every syndrome s, every i₀, j₀ and every E acting on ≤ ⌊(n−1)/2⌋ qubits, M = Q(s)Π_sEΠ_0 satisfies Π_0M = M, MX̄ = X̄M, MZ̄ = Z̄M; reason: on H_0, X̄ and Z̄ generate M₂(ℂ) on H^L, so an operator on H_0 commuting with both is G ⊗ I₂; every Kraus branch of the recovery then acts trivially on the encoded qubit
--   - generalization: "up to ⌊(n−1)/2⌋ arbitrary single qubit errors" → any operator acting on at most ⌊(n−1)/2⌋ qubits (QAlgorithms.ActsOnAtMost (m / 2)), entangling ones included; reason: every product of at most ⌊(n−1)/2⌋ single-qubit errors acts on at most that many qubits, so the source's claim is the special case
-- source:
--   D. Bacon, Operator Quantum Error Correcting Subsystems for Self-Correcting Quantum Memories, arXiv:quant-ph/0506023v4, §IV.C, p.8

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Defs_QECC
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_InT
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_InCS
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_weight
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_syndProj
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_recovery
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_Xbar
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_Zbar

open QAlgorithms

namespace BaconOQEC

/-- Bacon, *Operator Quantum Error Correcting Subsystems for Self-Correcting Quantum Memories*,
arXiv:quant-ph/0506023v4, §IV.C, p.8: "Thus the code we have constructed is a [n², n, 1] code: it
encodes a single qubit into n² qubits and has a distance n. … By the standard arguments of
digitizing errors in quantum error correcting codes, we have thus shown how our operator quantum
error correcting subsystem code can correct up to ⌊(n−1)/2⌋ arbitrary single qubit errors."
For the `n × n` lattice code with gauge group `T` (8) and stabilizer `S` (11), for every `n ≥ 1`:
1. (one encoded qubit) `T ⊆ C(S)` and `|C(S)| = 4 |T|`, where `C(S)` is the set of Pauli operators
   commuting with `S` (modulo phase);
2. (distance `n`) every Pauli operator in `C(S)` but not in `T` has weight at least `n`, and one of
   weight exactly `n` exists;
3. (correction of arbitrary errors on up to `⌊(n−1)/2⌋` qubits, modulo the gauge subsystem) for
   every syndrome `s`, every choice of the recovery's free row `i₀` and column `j₀`, and every operator
   `E` acting on at most `⌊(n−1)/2⌋` qubits, the branch `M = Q(s) Π_s E Π_0` of "error `E`, measure
   syndrome `s`, apply the recovery `Q(s)`" maps the code sector `H_0 = range Π_0` back into `H_0` and
   commutes with the logical operators `X̄` and `Z̄`, i.e. acts as `G ⊗ I₂` on
   `H_0 = H^T ⊗ H^L`: the encoded qubit is restored whatever happens to the gauge subsystem.

**Formalization Note.** `n = m + 1`, rows and columns 0-based, `⌊(n−1)/2⌋ = m / 2`. Clauses 1–2 work
with Pauli operators modulo phase (pairs of bit matrices; `C(S)` = `InCS`, `T` = `InT`); clause 3 uses
the actual matrices: `Π_s` = `syndProj s` (syndrome bit `0` = eigenvalue `+1`, code sector `s = 0`),
`Q(s)` = `recovery s i₀ j₀` (a function of the syndrome only), `X̄` = row `0` of `X`, `Z̄` = column `0` of
`Z`, and "acts on at most `t` qubits" is the published `QAlgorithms.ActsOnAtMost t` on the wires
`site i j`. On `H_0`, `X̄` and `Z̄` generate the logical `M₂(ℂ)`, whose commutant is `End(H^T) ⊗ I₂`. -/
theorem square_lattice_code (m : ℕ) :
    (∀ a b : Bits m, InT a b → InCS a b) ∧
    Nat.card {v : Bits m × Bits m // InCS v.1 v.2} =
      4 * Nat.card {v : Bits m × Bits m // InT v.1 v.2} ∧
    (∀ a b : Bits m, InCS a b → ¬ InT a b → m + 1 ≤ weight a b) ∧
    (∃ a b : Bits m, InCS a b ∧ ¬ InT a b ∧ weight a b = m + 1) ∧
    ∀ (s : (Fin m → ZMod 2) × (Fin m → ZMod 2)) (i0 j0 : Fin (m + 1))
      (E : Matrix (Qubits ((m + 1) * (m + 1))) (Qubits ((m + 1) * (m + 1))) ℂ),
      ActsOnAtMost (m / 2) E →
      let M := recovery s i0 j0 * syndProj s * E * syndProj 0
      syndProj 0 * M = M ∧ M * Xbar = Xbar * M ∧ M * Zbar = Zbar * M := by sorry

end BaconOQEC
